    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1043">

                <pb facs="1524" n="1508" sameAs="frus:frus1949v06/pg1508"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">867N.00/11–2349: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Egypt</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cairo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-11-23T13:00:00+02:00">November 23,
                            1949—1 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1094. Mytel 1060 November 14.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1043-f1"/> Hassan Youssef
                    told me last night that Shirine gave him Sassoon’s letter which he gave to the
                    King. The King said that he saw no object in dealing with Sassoon, for three <pb
                        facs="1525" n="1509" xml:id="pg1509"/> reasons: First, he does not know with
                    what authority Sassoon speaks; second, Sassoon is leaving soon for Ankara as
                    Israel representative; third, it is better in any event to wait for the
                    Department’s reply to the recent Arab note.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d1043-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>In connection with the third point I told Hassan Youssef to tell the King that in
                    my opinion the Arab suggestions were unrealistic and anything but helpful at
                    this juncture. He replied, “in that case what shall we do? Would it be possible
                    to proceed on the basis of the existing demarcation lines between Israel and the
                    Arabs?” He added quickly, “of course we could not admit to the other Arab states
                    that we are talking of this, but if your people thought well of it I think we
                    might get somewhere.” He added also that this was only his own suggestion.</p>
                <p>I said, “I can tell the State Department you suggested it”.</p>
                <p>Sent Department 1094, repeated Athens 33 for McGhee.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1043-f1" n="1" xml:id="d1043-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it advised that General Riley had brought a secret letter from Mr.
                    Sassoon “to Ismail Shirine King’s brother-in-law proposing meeting at El Auja
                    between representatives of the two parties to explore possibility reaching
                    general agreement between Egypt and Israel.” (767N.83/11–1449)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1043-f2" n="2" xml:id="d1043-f2-content">
                <p>Of November 14, <ref target="#pg1488">p. 1488</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
